remediation:
1. Implement strict parameter whitelisting - only accept and process explicitly defined parameters (username, password, email)
2. Reject or explicitly ignore any unexpected parameters with clear error messages
3. Use an allowlist/whitelist approach on the server side rather than blacklisting
4. Never use user input directly to set object properties or bulk assignment (avoid patterns like $user = $input;)
5. Explicitly assign parameters to object fields: $user->username = $input['username']; (not $user = $input;)
6. Validate parameter values against expected data types, ranges, and formats
7. Log suspicious parameter attempts for security monitoring and incident detection
8. Implement input validation framework to centralize parameter filtering
9. Use parameterized queries to prevent injection attacks
10. Apply principle of least privilege - only set attributes that should be settable during registration

Example secure implementation (PHP):
```php
$user = new User();
$user->setUsername($input['username']);
$user->setPassword($input['password']);
$user->setEmail($input['email']);
// Do NOT do: $user = (object)$input;
// Do NOT do: foreach($input as $key => $val) { $user->$key = $val; }
```
